From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:11:13 -0400
Subject: import: Arc's history and passwords, and one click for everything

Phase two of the Arc import (docs/features/import.md I6, I21-I23). Arc is a
Chromium, so its History and Login Data are Chromium's own SQLite files:
spaces::ArcDataImporter copies both into a temporary directory on the
thread pool (Arc holds the originals under an exclusive lock while it runs;
a hot journal beside the file rolls back on open), reads the urls table into
browsed history and the logins table into the profile's password store --
each value unsealed the way Chromium seals it on macOS, "v10" then
AES-128-CBC under PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 of the keychain password "Arc Safe
Storage" (salt "saltysalt", 1003 rounds, the RFC 6070 vectors in the test),
which macOS asks the user for once -- and writes through HistoryService and
the password store, the two calls Chromium's ProfileWriter makes. A refusal
at the keychain skips the passwords and the result line says so. Nothing
leaves the machine; the copies are deleted when the read ends.

The sidebar importer carries the data along: ArcImportOptions gains history
and passwords, on by default, so the welcome flow's Arc block is "Move
everything from Arc" with five checkboxes and one button, and the settings
row and the cmd-T action move everything too (I21, welcome W8).

Why: docs/ARC-ROUND2.md round 7, item 6: a switcher should move everything
from Arc in one click.
Removable when: Chromium's importer learns Chromium-family profiles (a
TYPE_CHROMIUM source with a per-app keychain item).
